22.3  |\n| OpenAI o3          |52.5|42.4    |22.8| 22.7    |22.1  |22.3  |\n| OpenAI o3+Thinking |52.5|49.3    |23.6| 22.7    |22.1  |22.3  |\n| DeepSeek V3        |52.4|41.5    |18.1| 22.7    |22.1  |22.3  |\n| DeepSeek R1        |50.6|40.8    |19.5| 22.7    |22.1  |22.3  |\n| Self-RAG           |60.6|41.4    |28.1| 39.5    |38.7  |39.1  |\n| SeakR              |57.3|42.0    |27.9| 36.6    |36.3  |37.2  |\n| Multi-hop RAG      |42.1|39.4    |20.1| 16.5    |16.3  |14.4  |\n| Search-R1          |63.1|40.0    |38.0| 42.5    |41.1  |43.9  |\n| R-Search           |60.4|38.9    |35.6| 40.8    |40.6  |42.3  |\n| Re-Call            |61.3|37.5    |33.7| 40.6    |40.4  |40.8  |\n\nAs shown in the table, the trends match with our conclusion in the paper that Standard RAG scheme cannot directly solve the problem, and Multi-hop RAG scheme has better performance than Standard RAG scheme. Interleaving RAG provides the best pipeline performance."},"title":{"value":"Response to Weakness 3"}},"parentInvitations":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Official_Comment","tmdate":1770917757437,"tcdate":1764051290679,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission24399/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission24399/Authors"],"forum":"ddpLHL9JJo","number":10,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1764051290679,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission24399/-/Official_Comment","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770917757437,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","replyto":"XehNWZeYwQ","id":"GMDKAeGG03","forumContent":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"keywords":{"value":["Iterative Reasoning","Document Retrieval","Benchmark"]},"primary_area":{"value":"datasets and benchmarks"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities\nacross a wide spectrum of natural language tasks, especially information retrieval\nand comprehensive reasoning. However, existing benchmarks typically evaluate\nthese abilities dependently, failing to capture the process with interleaving and in-\ntegrated reasoning and retrieval for complex queries, which is commonly needed\nin real-world applications. To address this limitation, we propose a novel bench-\nmark—MIRAGE—the first benchmark specifically designed to assess an LLM’s\nability of step-wise reasoning on a decomposed query and iterative retrieval based\non intermediate reasoning outcomes across multiple interactions, ultimately syn-\nthesizing a coherent and comprehensive answer. MIRAGE consists of 579 real-\nworld queries spanning diverse domain including Finance, Legal, Technology,\nAcademia. We collected both open-sourced dataset and real-world forum con-\nversation as our data sources, and we further curate the dataset into a multi-turn\nformat along with a consicse question and comprehensive answer. To support fur-\nther development and scalability, we also introduce a data generation pipeline for\nbenchmark expansion. We evaluate state-of-the-art approaches on the our bench-\nmark and find that none exhibit consistently strong performance, underscoring\ntheir lack of ability of interleaving reasoning and retrieval as well as the need for\nfurther advancement. Our benchmark provides a foundation for future research\ninto more robust and dynamic information-seeking agents."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nwang2026mirage,\ntitle={{MIRAGE}: {MULTI}-{HOP} {INTERLEAVED} {REASONING} {AND} {RETRIEVAL}-{GROUNDED} {EVIDENCE}},\nauthor={Quansen Wang and Jiaqi Li and Zixia Jia and Song-Chun Zhu and Di He and Zilong Zheng},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ddpLHL9JJo}\n}"},"title":{"value":"MIRAGE: MULTI-HOP INTERLEAVED REASONING AND RETRIEVAL-GROUNDED EVIDENCE"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e437df5b72f7e0274d4c6311c0dd6b00042bd1db.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|mirage_multihop_interleaved_reasoning_and_retrievalgrounded_evidence"},"authorids":{"value":["~Quansen_Wang1","~Jiaqi_Li10","~Zixia_Jia1","~Song-Chun_Zhu1","~Di_He1","~Zilong_Zheng1"]},"authors":{"value":["Quansen Wang","Jiaqi Li","Zixia Jia","Song-Chun Zhu","Di He","Zilong Zheng"]}},"version":2},{"content":{"comment":{"value":"We sincerely thank you for your continued engagement and for acknowledging our cost comparison analysis as a \"plus to the paper.\" We are encouraged that the efficiency advantages of our method are clear.\n\nRegarding your concern about the technical novelty in light of *Jiang et al. (2025) [s3]*, we will explicitly incorporate this work into our Related Works section in the final revision. However, we respectfully submit that characterizing SIT as incremental to s3 overlooks fundamental divergences in system architecture, learning paradigms, and reasoning capabilities.\n\n**1. Structural Divergence**\n\ns3 and SIT represent different agentic structures. s3 is essentially a **Recursive Retrieval module** paired with a passive generator, whereas SIT is a **Unified Reasoning Agent**.\n*   **s3 (Search-Select-Serve Loop):** As shown in their Figure 4, s3 performs a loop strictly within the search phase. The search agent cannot answer the question; its sole function is to prepare a context buffer. Once the search loop finishes, the context is passed to a frozen LLM for a single-step generation. This is a pipeline, not an interleaved reasoning process.\n*   **SIT (Interleaved Think-Search Loop):** SIT integrates \"Thinking\" (Reasoning) and \"Searching\" into a single, unified policy.  Unlike s3, our model preserves its reasoning state across steps, allowing it to adjust its plan based on new information.\n\n**2. Learning Paradigm**\n*   **s3 (Modular/Frozen):** s3 trains a searcher to feed a frozen black-box generator. It optimizes inputs, not reasoning. Consequently, s3 never faces the \"exploration-exploitation\" challenges of training a reasoning model.\n*   **SIT (End-to-End/Joint):** SIT trains the LLM itself to reason and retrieve jointly. The \"Search-Initialized\" mechanism is a theoretical intervention for RL dynamics (as detailed in our Entropy Analysis). It solves the \"hallucinated planning\" problem where an agent attempts to plan reasoning paths without grounding—a critical issue in training end-to-end agents that s3 simply avoids by not training the generator at all.\n\n**3. Limitations in Multi-hop Reasoning**\n\ns3's reliance on a frozen generator severely limits its ability to handle complex multi-hop reasoning. Since the generator is not updated, it struggles to decompose and reason over retrieved contexts precisely. This is evidenced by s3's own results: in their Table 1, while they claim high \"Generation Accuracy\" (via LLM Judge), their **Exact Match (EM)** scores are drastically lower than end-to-end baselines like Search-R1.\n*   *Evidence:* On HotpotQA, s3 achieves only **21.8% EM**, compared to Search-R1's **43.8%**. On Musique, s3 drops to **6.1% EM**, while Search-R1 maintains **17.2%**.\nThis performance gap confirms that without end-to-end training, the system fails to achieve precise, rigorous reasoning. **According to our results (Table 2 & 4), SIT further significantly improves upon Search-R1 (e.g., +10.91 EM on HotpotQA, +15.63 EM on Musique).** Consequently, SIT demonstrates superior performance across the board on these standard, rigorous benchmarks compared to the modular approach of s3.\n\n**Summary**\n\nTo classify SIT as incremental to s3 is to conflate \"building a better search tool\" with \"training a better reasoning brain.\" s3 optimizes a search loop for a fixed model; SIT advances the state-of-the-art in training agents that can *think* and *search* synergistically via RL."}},"parentInvitations":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Official_Comment","tmdate":1764150995163,"tcdate":1764150995163,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission522/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission522/Authors"],"forum":"kK2JT3PC1N","number":10,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1764150995163,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission522/-/Official_Comment"],"mdate":1764150995163,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","replyto":"eqXZNRC5BK","id":"ozgwZn7w4y","forumContent":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["RAG; Reinforcement Learning"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/7def73820eaaad7a379087ec1c80c012b9697233.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"applications to computer vision, audio, language, and other modalities"},"abstract":{"value":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a powerful paradigm for Large Language Models (LLMs) to address knowledge-intensive queries requiring domain-specific or up-to-date information. To handle complex multi-hop questions that are challenging for single-step retrieval, iterative RAG approaches incorporating reinforcement learning have been proposed. However, existing iterative RAG systems typically \\textit{think first} to decompose questions without leveraging information about the available retrieval corpus, leading to inefficient retrieval and reasoning chains that cascade into suboptimal performance. In this paper, we introduce Search-Initialized Thinking (SIT), a novel framework that \\textit{searches first} before think in iterative RAG systems with contextually relevant retrieved knowledge. From an entropy perspective, we demonstrate that incorporating initial knowledge with search reduces unnecessary exploration during the reasoning process, enabling the model to focus more effectively on relevant information subsets. Extensive experiments on six standard RAG datasets demonstrate that by establishing a stronger reasoning foundation, SIT significantly improves retrieval precision, reduces cascading errors, and enhances both performance and efficiency. Moreover, SIT proves effective as a versatile, training-free inference strategy that scales seamlessly to large models.Generalization tests across diverse datasets and retrieval corpora confirm the robustness of our approach. Overall, SIT advances the state-of-the-art in iterative RAG systems while illuminating the critical interplay between structured reasoning and efficient exploration in reinforcement learning-augmented frameworks."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nwang2026search,\ntitle={Search or Think? Rethinking Iterative {RAG} from An Entropy Perspective},\nauthor={Yuxin Wang and Shicheng Fang and Bo Wang and Qi Luo and Xuanjing Huang and Yining Zheng and Xipeng Qiu},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=kK2JT3PC1N}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Search or Think? Rethinking Iterative RAG from An Entropy Perspective"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ae43a8db1286d41de13977b8b6ddf9a0bee71f3b.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|search_or_think_rethinking_iterative_rag_from_an_entropy_perspective"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yuxin_Wang3","~Shicheng_Fang1","~Bo_Wang38","~Qi_Luo6","~Xuanjing_Huang1","~Yining_Zheng1","~Xipeng_Qiu1"]},"authors":{"value":["Yuxin Wang","Shicheng Fang","Bo Wang","Qi Luo","Xuanjing Huang","Yining Zheng","Xipeng Qiu"]}},"version":2},{"content":{"comment":{"value":"**Q3:**\n> Please add head-to-head ablations or analysis vs. recent “when-to-think / routing” methods and hybrid models (e.g., LHRM (Jiang et al., 2025), BPO (Yang et al., 2025b), AdaCoT (Lou et al., 2025), Search-R1 (Jin et al., 2025)). What incremental gain comes specifically from (i) three-way routing, (ii) the adaptive penalty, and (iii) prefix-enforced exploration?\n\n**Response:**\n\nThank you for pointing out the importance of head-to-head analysis with existing methods.  Below we summarize the 3 key differences and the incremental gains introduced by A²FM.\n\n**1. High-level comparison**\n\n| Feature                   | **A²FM (ours)**                                                | LHRM                                 | BPO                                  | AdaCoT                          | Search-R1              |\n|---------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|---------------------------------|------------------------|\n| **Routing space**         | **3-way:** instant / reasoning / agentic (search, crawl, code) | think / non-think                    | think / non-think                    | think / non-think               | agentic (search only)  |\n| **RL reward strategy**    | **Per-query adaptive reward**                | intra-/inter-group reward norm       | /                                    | penalty-based thinking tradeoff      | /                      |\n| **RL sampling strategy**  | **Prefix-enforced multi-mode rollouts**                        | balanced sampling, no routing reason | balanced sampling, no routing reason | /                               | /                      |\n\n**2. Incremental gains from the three-way routing space**\n\nExisting work is typically limited to binary routing (think vs non-think) or is restricted to one modality (search-only agents). In contrast, **A²FM unifies three execution behaviors within a single policy and router**, enabling broad generalization across instant, reasoning, and tool-augmented agentic tasks.\n\nOther methods lack such coverage: \n\n- LHRM, BPO, and AdaCoT cannot generate tool-using trajectories and thus cannot engage agentic benchmarks;  \n- Search-R1 excels at search but has limited reasoning and instant capabilities.\n\nWe also validate this with additional controlled ablations:\n\n- \"auto-think\": we force reasoning and instant modes for each query, counting as solved if either mode succeeds.\n- \"auto-agentic\": similar to the above, but using agentic and instant modes instead.\n\n| Model Variant                  | GAIA     | AIME24   |\n|------------------------------  |----------|----------|\n| auto-think  | 22.3     | -        |\n| auto-agentic  | -        | 67.2     |\n| **A²FM**                       | **57.3** | **74.5** |\n\nThese results show that neither a pure \"auto-think\" nor a pure \"auto-agentic\" strategy can approximate the performance of three-way adaptive routing.\n\n**3. Incremental gains from the per-query adaptive reward**\n\nA²FM employs an **adaptive reward** that conditions the cost penalty on each query’s difficulty:  \n\n- if instant mode is sufficient, non-instant modes receive a penalty;  \n- if instant fails, no penalty is applied and the router is encouraged to route to more expressive modes.\n\nThis yields **fine-grained, query-level routing supervision**, unlike existing methods:\n\n- BPO / Search-R1 don't employ any per-query difficulty awareness.  \n- LHRM reduces cross-group reward bias by normalization but still lacks per-query adaptivity.  \n- AdaCoT uses two fixed hyperparameters for over/under-triggering regardless of the query’s inherent difficulty.\n\nWe also have conducted an ablation on SuperGPQA:\n\n| Variant                     | Score      | Instant Mode Rate |\n|-----------------------------|------------|-------------------|\n| w/o adaptive reward         | 55.6       | 50.2              |\n| **A²FM adaptive reward**    | 54.7       | 58.6              |\n\nWe observe that the A²FM will chose the instant mode on easy queries while still routing to reasoning or agentic behaviors on harder ones. Notably, the overall score decreases only slightly, but the instant-mode usage increases substantially, indicating that **the adaptive reward effectively encourages efficiency without sacrificing much accuracy.**\n\nDue to the response length limit, we continue our answer below. (Response continued.)"}},"parentInvitations":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Official_Comment","tmdate":1764060312113,"tcdate":1763540597712,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5384/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5384/Authors"],"forum":"3kvV1nfWVq","number":12,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1763540597712,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5384/-/Official_Comment"],"mdate":1764060312113,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","replyto":"D84DJCiadI","id":"othIn5S39r","forumContent":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose A²FM, a unified 32B model combining agentic, reasoning, and instant modes via adaptive routing and APO, achieving state-of-the-art accuracy with substantially improved cost efficiency."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Adaptive LLMs","Deep Research","Agent Reasoning"]},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models split into two families: reasoning-centric LLMs, which strengthen internal chain-of-thought reasoning but cannot invoke external tools, and agentic LLMs, which learn to interact with environments and leverage tools but often lag in deep reasoning. This divide arises from fundamentally different training objectives, leading to mismatched strengths and inefficiency on simple queries, where both families tend to overthink or over-call tools. In this work, we present Adaptive Agent Foundation Model (A$^2$FM), a unified framework that follows a route-then-align principle: the model first learns task-aware routing and then aligns mode-specific trajectories under a shared backbone. To address the inefficiency gap, we introduce a third instant mode that handles simple queries directly, preventing unnecessary reasoning or tool calls while complementing the agentic and reasoning modes. To jointly enhance accuracy and efficiency, we propose Adaptive Policy Optimization (APO), which enforces adaptive sampling across modes and applies a cost-regularized reward. On the 32B scale, A$^2$FM achieves 13.4\\% on BrowseComp, 70.4\\% on AIME25, and 16.7\\% on HLE, setting new SOTA among comparable models and performing competitively with frontier LLMs across agentic, reasoning, and general benchmarks. Notably, the adaptive execution achieves a cost of pass of only \\$0.00487 per correct answer—cutting cost by 45.2\\% relative to reasoning and 33.5\\% relative to agentic, thus delivering substantially higher cost efficiency while maintaining comparable accuracy."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nchen2026afm,\ntitle={A\\${\\textasciicircum}2\\${FM}: An Adaptive Agent Foundation Model for Tool-Aware Hybrid Reasoning},\nauthor={Qianben Chen and Jingyi Cao and Jiayu Zhang and Tianrui Qin and LiXiaowan and King Zhu and Dingfeng Shi and He Zhu and Minghao Liu and Xiaobo Liang and Ge Zhang and Jian Yang and Yuchen Eleanor Jiang and Wangchunshu Zhou},\nbooktitle={The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=3kvV1nfWVq}\n}"},"title":{"value":"A$^2$FM: An Adaptive Agent Foundation Model for Tool-Aware Hybrid Reasoning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/fac0825b4f071681eae9afd727d468012c761e35.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|a^2fm_an_adaptive_agent_foundation_model_for_toolaware_hybrid_reasoning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Qianben_Chen1","~Jingyi_Cao3","~Jiayu_Zhang8","~Tianrui_Qin1","~LiXiaowan1","~King_Zhu2","~Dingfeng_Shi1","~He_Zhu8","~Minghao_Liu11","~Xiaobo_Liang2","~Ge_Zhang5","~Jian_Yang10","~Yuchen_Eleanor_Jiang1","~Wangchunshu_Zhou1"]},"authors":{"value":["Qianben Chen","Jingyi Cao","Jiayu Zhang","Tianrui Qin","LiXiaowan","King Zhu","Dingfeng Shi","He Zhu","Minghao Liu","Xiaobo Liang","Ge Zhang","Jian Yang","Yuchen Eleanor Jiang","Wangchunshu Zhou"]}},"version":2},{"content":{"comment":{"value":"We thank the reviewer for the careful reading and valuable feedback on our work, and we greatly appreciate the comments!\n\n> W1: The theoretical guarantee relies on a strict MDP structure (deterministic, tree-structured, binary terminal rewards). While standard for current math benchmarks, this may not hold for more complex reasoning tasks involving tool use, intermediate feedback, or stochastic environments, potentially limiting broader applicability.\n\nThanks for the question. We would like to clarify why our framework has a broader application than it might initially appear:\n\n### Natural properties of LLM reasoning tasks (not strict limitations) & Additional theoretical results\n\nThe key MDP properties (deterministic transition dynamics and tree structure) in our theory are not limiting assumptions but rather reflect the inherent nature of general RLVR tasks with LLM reasoning (math, code, tool use as in Search-R1 [1]) with verifiable rewards, while we have also extended our theory to incorporate intermediate feedback to further broaden its scope.\n\n1. **Transition dynamics**: This property naturally emerges from token-level action definition in LLM reasoning in RLVR tasks. **Even with tools that appear stochastic (e.g., search engines), the MDP with token-level action definition still has deterministic transition dynamics.** Consider a search tool example in Search-R1 [1] (or similar tool-use setups): when an LLM queries a search engine within its reasoning process, the search results may appear \"random\" or unpredictable. However, based on the MDP formulation with token-level action, the search result (whether successful or failed) becomes part of the state once received, and the next token generation is deterministic conditioned on this state.\n\n**Example**: Given a query \"... \\</search>\", if the search engine responds \"This is 1\", then:\nP(next_state=\"... \\</search>\\<information> This\" | state=\"... \\</search>\\<information>\", action=\"This\")=1. Similarly, for a failed search (with response \"Fail\"):\nP(next_state=\"... \\</search>\\<information> Fail\" | state=\"... \\</search><\\information>\", action=\"Fail\")=1\n\nThe key insight is that in the MDP (with token-level action definition), the agent's actions are individual tokens it generates, not the API calls themselves. The source of uncertainty (external tool) becomes fixed information in the state (analogous to the randomness outcomes and deterministic mappings in random variables). This applies to various LLM reasoning scenarios with verifiable rewards (e.g., Search-R1 [1]). We have conducted additional experiments based on Search-R1 [1] as below, which demonstrates ROVER's effectiveness.\n    \n2. **Tree structure**: This directly follows from autoregressive language generation, where each token choice (action) creates a new branch (next state) in the reasoning path, and the sequence can never cycle back to a previous state (unlike navigation or control problems), which holds universally across all reasoning domains based on autoregressive generation.\n    \n3. **Reward structure**: We follow current popular practices in reasoning tasks with verifiable rewards that are based on outcome rewards [2,3,4], as the correctness is evaluated upon completion, and this kind of reward signal is already effective to guide the agent's RL training. \n\nAlthough current benchmarks typically use outcome-based terminal rewards, we have extended our theoretical results to accommodate intermediate feedback (we included the proof and detailed discussion in Appendix A.3):"},"title":{"value":"Response to Reviewer Qj2i (Part 1)"}},"parentInvitations":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Official_Comment","tmdate":1763729137858,"tcdate":1763729116561,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission10272/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission10272/Authors"],"forum":"ujLgLz6QQa","number":2,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1763729116561,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission10272/-/Official_Comment"],"mdate":1763729137858,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","replyto":"a4Sa5uiAFS","id":"6OqnL6gHSU","forumContent":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Poster"},"TLDR":{"value":"A minimalist yet highly effective RL method that maintains both quality and diversity for LLM reasoning"},"keywords":{"value":["Large Language Models (LLMs)","Reinforcement Learning","RLVR","Math Reasoning","Diversity"]},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"RL with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has emerged as a promising paradigm for improving the reasoning abilities of large language models (LLMs). Current methods rely primarily on policy optimization frameworks like PPO and GRPO, which follow generalized policy iteration that alternates between evaluating the current policy's value and improving the policy based on evaluation. While effective, they often suffer from training instability and diversity collapse, requiring complex heuristic tricks and careful tuning. We observe that standard RLVR in math reasoning can be formalized as a specialized finite-horizon Markov Decision Process with deterministic state transitions, tree-structured dynamics, and binary terminal rewards. Though large in scale, the underlying structure is simpler than general-purpose control settings for which popular RL algorithms (e.g., PPO) were developed, suggesting that several sophisticated techniques in existing methods may be reduced or even omitted. Based on this insight, we prove a surprising result: the optimal action can be recovered from the Q-function of a fixed uniformly random policy, thereby bypassing the generalized policy iteration loop and its associated heuristics. We introduce \\underline{\\textbf{R}}andom P\\underline{\\textbf{o}}licy \\underline{\\textbf{V}}aluation for Div\\underline{\\textbf{e}}rse \\underline{\\textbf{R}}easoning (ROVER) to translate this principle into a practical and scalable algorithm for LLM math reasoning, a minimalist yet highly effective RL method that samples actions from a softmax over these uniform-policy Q-values. ROVER preserves diversity throughout training, allowing sustained exploration of multiple valid pathways. Across multiple base models and standard math reasoning benchmarks, ROVER demonstrates superior performance in both \\textbf{quality} (\\textbf{+8.2} on pass@1, \\textbf{+16.8} on pass@256) and \\textbf{diversity} (\\textbf{+20.5\\%}), despite its radical simplification compared to strong, complicated existing methods."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nhe2026random,\ntitle={Random Policy Valuation is Enough for {LLM} Reasoning with Verifiable Rewards},\nauthor={Haoran He and Yuxiao Ye and Qingpeng Cai and Chen Hu and Binxing Jiao and Daxin Jiang and Ling Pan},\nbooktitle={The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ujLgLz6QQa}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Random Policy Valuation is Enough for LLM Reasoning with Verifiable Rewards"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3350201c928d634955c4d0d9896a962dc1c8396d.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"he|random_policy_valuation_is_enough_for_llm_reasoning_with_verifiable_rewards"},"authorids":{"value":["~Haoran_He1","~Yuxiao_Ye3","~Qingpeng_Cai2","~Chen_Hu1","~Binxing_Jiao1","~Daxin_Jiang2","~Ling_Pan1"]},"authors":{"value":["Haoran He","Yuxiao Ye","Qingpeng Cai","Chen Hu","Binxing Jiao","Daxin Jiang","Ling Pan"]}},"version":2},{"content":{"comment":{"value":"**Q3:**\n> Please add head-to-head ablations or analysis vs. recent “when-to-think / routing” methods and hybrid models (e.g., LHRM (Jiang et al., 2025), BPO (Yang et al., 2025b), AdaCoT (Lou et al., 2025), Search-R1 (Jin et al., 2025)). What incremental gain comes specifically from (i) three-way routing, (ii) the adaptive penalty, and (iii) prefix-enforced exploration?\n\n**Continuation of our response to Q3:**\n\n**4. Incremental gains from the prefix-enforced multi-mode sampling**\n\nA²FM is the **first** model that applies both equal sampling for every mode and adaptive sampling in the same time:\n\n- Equal sampling for every mode (using prefix-enforced forced-mode technique) . For each query, we generate 3 forced-instant, 3 forced-reasoning, and 3 forced-agentic rollouts.  This provides **unbiased mode-conditioned estimates** for every input, enabling the router to learn how accuracy varies across modes on the *same input*.\n\n- Adaptive sampling (joint learning for routing and aligning). Forced rollouts teach within-mode alignment but do not teach routing; In the contrast, the adaptive rollout ensures training matches inference behavior and jointly optimizes routing and trajectory generation.\n\nOther methods lack this two-part design:  \n\n- LHRM/BPO perform balanced sampling but do not explictly learn the routing mechanism;\n- AdaCoT trains a single PPO policy;\n- Search-R1 only explores within search, not over multiple global behaviors.\n\nTogether, these two sampling components allow A²FM to learn both **which mode** to choose and **how** to execute that mode.\n\nWe hope this clarifies the incremental contributions introduced by our design, and we appreciate your suggestions.\n\n**References:**\n[1] Lingjie Jiang, Xun Wu, Shaohan Huang, Qingxiu Dong, Zewen Chi, Li Dong, Xingxing Zhang, Tengchao\nLv, Lei Cui, and Furu Wei. Think only when you need with large hybrid-reasoning models, 2025. URL\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14631.\n\n[2] Qi Yang, Bolin Ni, Shiming Xiang, Han Hu, Houwen Peng, and Jie Jiang. R-4b: Incentivizing general-purpose\nauto-thinking capability in mllms via bi-mode annealing and reinforce learning, 2025b. URL https://\narxiv.org/abs/2508.21113.\n\n[3] Chenwei Lou, Zewei Sun, Xinnian Liang, Meng Qu, Wei Shen, Wenqi Wang, Yuntao Li, Qingping Yang, and\nShuangzhi Wu. Adacot: Pareto-optimal adaptive chain-of-thought triggering via reinforcement learning.\narXiv preprint arXiv:2505.11896, 2025.\n\n[4] Bowen Jin, Hansi Zeng, Zhenrui Yue, Jinsung Yoon, Sercan Arik, Dong Wang, Hamed Zamani, and Jiawei\nHan. Search-r1: Training llms to reason and leverage search engines with reinforcement learning. arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2503.09516, 2025.\n\n[5] Lingjie Jiang, Xun Wu, Shaohan Huang, Qingxiu Dong, Zewen Chi, Li Dong, Xingxing Zhang, Tengchao\nLv, Lei Cui, and Furu Wei. Think only when you need with large hybrid-reasoning models, 2025. URL\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14631.\n\n[6] Qi Yang, Bolin Ni, Shiming Xiang, Han Hu, Houwen Peng, and Jie Jiang. R-4b: Incentivizing general-purpose\nauto-thinking capability in mllms via bi-mode annealing and reinforce learning, 2025b. URL https://\narxiv.org/abs/2508.21113.\n\n[7] Jin B, Zeng H, Yue Z, et al. Search-r1: Training llms to reason and leverage search engines with reinforcement learning[J]. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.09516, 2025."}},"parentInvitations":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Official_Comment","tmdate":1763540663489,"tcdate":1763540663489,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5384/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5384/Authors"],"forum":"3kvV1nfWVq","number":13,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1763540663489,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5384/-/Official_Comment"],"mdate":1763540663489,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","replyto":"othIn5S39r","id":"4mrKGdldLo","forumContent":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose A²FM, a unified 32B model combining agentic, reasoning, and instant modes via adaptive routing and APO, achieving state-of-the-art accuracy with substantially improved cost efficiency."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Adaptive LLMs","Deep Research","Agent Reasoning"]},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models split into two families: reasoning-centric LLMs, which strengthen internal chain-of-thought reasoning but cannot invoke external tools, and agentic LLMs, which learn to interact with environments and leverage tools but often lag in deep reasoning. This divide arises from fundamentally different training objectives, leading to mismatched strengths and inefficiency on simple queries, where both families tend to overthink or over-call tools. In this work, we present Adaptive Agent Foundation Model (A$^2$FM), a unified framework that follows a route-then-align principle: the model first learns task-aware routing and then aligns mode-specific trajectories under a shared backbone. To address the inefficiency gap, we introduce a third instant mode that handles simple queries directly, preventing unnecessary reasoning or tool calls while complementing the agentic and reasoning modes. To jointly enhance accuracy and efficiency, we propose Adaptive Policy Optimization (APO), which enforces adaptive sampling across modes and applies a cost-regularized reward. On the 32B scale, A$^2$FM achieves 13.4\\% on BrowseComp, 70.4\\% on AIME25, and 16.7\\% on HLE, setting new SOTA among comparable models and performing competitively with frontier LLMs across agentic, reasoning, and general benchmarks. Notably, the adaptive execution achieves a cost of pass of only \\$0.00487 per correct answer—cutting cost by 45.2\\% relative to reasoning and 33.5\\% relative to agentic, thus delivering substantially higher cost efficiency while maintaining comparable accuracy."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nchen2026afm,\ntitle={A\\${\\textasciicircum}2\\${FM}: An Adaptive Agent Foundation Model for Tool-Aware Hybrid Reasoning},\nauthor={Qianben Chen and Jingyi Cao and Jiayu Zhang and Tianrui Qin and LiXiaowan and King Zhu and Dingfeng Shi and He Zhu and Minghao Liu and Xiaobo Liang and Ge Zhang and Jian Yang and Yuchen Eleanor Jiang and Wangchunshu Zhou},\nbooktitle={The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=3kvV1nfWVq}\n}"},"title":{"value":"A$^2$FM: An Adaptive Agent Foundation Model for Tool-Aware Hybrid Reasoning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/fac0825b4f071681eae9afd727d468012c761e35.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"I